A||N||20||owenslee||Opinions Wanted : Muncie vs.Getrag w. 3.4L||||7||
Z||000000||owenslee||03-22-2001||10:33 PM||||I would appreciate opinions from those currently driving 3.4L pushrod engines in Fiero's with either the 4 spd muncie or 5 spd Getrag.<P>I fully appreciate the fact that the Getrag is stronger and normally a 5 spd will allow keeping an engine closer to torque peak with improved RPM recovery after a shift...<P>But...I would like some real world experiences as to driveability and other issues.<P>In a 3.4 which has more torque than the 2.8  the 4 spd would pay less of a penalty in performance and might offer some advantages in final axle ratio and transmission gear ratio spread.<P>I have an early 86 GT with the 4 spd Muncie that will soon be recieving a Grooms 3.4 long block. I also have an 87 GT subframe with a low mileage Fiero Getrag. I lack the shifter and cables for the Getrag.<P>Is there sufficient advantage to the Getrag to make the swap?  Transmission strength is not an issue but other judgement criteria MIGHT warrant making the switch while the engine is out.<P>If any of you have any computer programs such as "Quarter" I would appreciate an estimation of 0-60 and 0-1320 differences between the two transmission given 180HP and 205 Ft Lbs torque.<P>Any input is appreciated from experienced Fiero owners.<P>Kerry<P>------------------<BR>"Badges?...Badges..??? We don gat to sho yu no stinkin' BADGES..!!!"<p>[This message has been edited by owenslee (edited 03-23-2001).]||||reg||7
Z||000001||MooCow||03-22-2001||11:44 PM||||I have an 84 4-speed tranny with my 3.4.  I wanted the 84 because it had a 4:10 gear ratio.  Let me tell you it is very fast off the line.  Highway driving isn't to bad either. At 70 the RPM's are around 4 I believe.  I don't have a clue what a 5-speed is like sorry.<P>                              Thanks Ken<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/kensig_1.jpg"> <BR>87 GT, 3.4 engine with a 4:10 tranny.  Soon will be Viper Yellow!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Standard||03-23-2001||12:22 AM||||  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">4k at 70?!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">  wow, I'm only turning around 2400 at 70.. don't even get close to 4k in 5th until I'm above 90...<P>The main problem with the 5 speed getrag is finding one.  They're not the most plentiful tranny's and one in good shape will cost a fair bit of change.  And they have issues with synchro's, mainly 3rd gear (mine's shot).  From what I've heard, the v6 4 speed is the strongest of all the Fiero tranny's.  It has a 3.65 final drive ratio, so you won't be buzzing too high on the freeway.<p>[This message has been edited by Standard (edited 03-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Formula88||03-23-2001||09:44 AM||||Yes, but ANY FWD 5-speed Getrag can be made to work in a Fiero.  Rodney Dickman has conversion kits that bolt on to the outside of the tranny (no internal work necessary) to make the linkages hook up.  I will be considering this if I need a replacement for my Getrag in the future.  Although, I'm currently looking for a spare Getrag to rebuild and keep on hand in case LZeitgeist or I grenade a tranny (heaven forbid).||||reg||4
Z||000004||owenslee||03-23-2001||11:54 AM||||Keep in mind guys that I HAVE a spare Getrag 5 spd on a 87 Fiero Subframe now. Locating or converting a non Fiero Getrag is not the issue.<P>I am looking for convincing arguments to KEEP the 4 spd Muncie with the 3.4L or convincing arguments to install the 5 spd Getrag...<P>Right now I am leaning toward installing the 4.10 final drive gear from an 84 se 4 cyl into my v-6 4 spd Muncie for increased acceleration. <P>The only downside I see presently over the Getrag is that I lose the .72  O.D 5th gear  for reduced RPM at cruise.<P>Others might argue that the 4.10 final with a 3.31 first gear (13.57 overall) might be too low for the increased torgue of the 3.4 resulting in excess tire spin off the line. But again I'm looking for personal experience here...not just speculation on my part.<P>Although some believe the Getrag is the strongest of the two transmissions ( backed up by GM maximum  torque ratings) ...others judge the Muncie to be  more durable than the Getrag 5 spd  in day to day driving      ( considering the potentially weak syncros of the Getrag 5 spd), ....at the expense of slighly higher shift effort on the Muncie 4 spd.<P>I understand Ed Parks went with  a modified V-6 Muncie 4 spd  with the 4.10 final drive gear on a 3.4L and is very impressed with the combination, but I've not discussed it with him directly.<P>Any other opinions...?<P>Thanks.<P>||||reg||7
Z||000005||WKDFIRO||03-23-2001||01:08 PM||||It also depends on what you are looking for. <P>Having driven a 3.4 with 4.10 gears in a 4 speed on a long trip (I was Keith Huffs drive partner for a Fiero show up north) I realized that as a long trip car the 4 speed is just not up to it. I missed my former Fiero (before the accident) that had the 5th gear. In the real world of freeway speed the 5th gear is crucial. <P>Now if you are just going around town, or don't do long trips that much than the 4 speed would do great utilizing the torque of the 3.4. Even now I catch myself putting in the clutch and looking for a 5th gear that isn't there in my 85 GT. The 3.4 has enough torque to make a 5 speed just as much fun as a 4 speed (no downshifting into 4th to pass!) and yet you have the option of putting it into 5th when you are not feeling like a lane jockey.<P>Bottom line. You want performance only, stay with 4 gears. You want it to be friendly and sociable then find the cables and hook up the 5 speed. Having lots of experience with both and currently living with a 4 speed I would go with 5th gear. <P>Im not always racing.||||reg||1
Z||000006||elfiero||03-23-2001||01:21 PM||||The 4spd will make the car a pocket rocket-<BR>NOBODY will beat you away from a stoplite.<BR>As always there is a BUT,after about a month<BR>of general purpose driving,you WILL say to<BR>yourself,damm,I wish I had a 5spd.Again,if<BR>your gonna parkinglot race it,or street race<BR>it and never go on the highway,by all means<BR>use the 4spd.Anything else-5spd ONLY.<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000007||Cooter||03-23-2001||01:47 PM||||I have the 4.10's and a 3.4. I like it because of the killer acceleration. I got a good deal on my 4 speed, but if it ever goes out, then I will build an Isuzu 5 speed with a 3.73 gear set. That would be a better combination of acceleration vs. economy.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Will||03-23-2001||02:24 PM||||Has anyone actually compared a 3.4 4spd vs a 3.4 5 spd? I bet the 5spd would leave the 4 spd on the highway (4th gear is lower). I really don't see how first gear in a Getrag could be any lower (3.50x3.61=12.64) and still be useful.<BR>Go to <A HREF="http://www.mantapart.com" TARGET=_blank>www.mantapart.com</A>  and get a 3.94 gearset for you Getrag.<P>I find myself wishing for a taller top gear in my Getrag a lot more often than I find myself wishing for a lower first gear.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Will (edited 03-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||88formula||03-23-2001||07:56 PM||||Here's mine. I ran some numbers for you with Dragstrip2000 using a stock 3.4-liter Camaro engine.<P>Four speed Muncie with 3.65 final drive I got 0-60 mph in 6.56 seconds and the quarter mile in 14.67 seconds @ 95.3 mph.<P>Five speed Getrag with 3.61 final drive I got 0-60 mph in 6.54 seconds and the quarter mile in 14.62 seconds @ 94.2 mph.<P>Four speed Muncie with 4.10 final drive I got 0-60 mph in 6.53 seconds and the quarter mile in 14.69 seconds @ 94.2 mph.<P>The Five speed Getrag shows the best 60-foot time of 2.177 seconds compared to 2.263 for the Muncie and the 3.65 final drive and 2.210 for the Muncie with the 4.10 final drive. <P>My opinion is that the Five-speed transmission is the best one because of the fifth overdrive gear and it gives the best overall performance with that motor. I have the five-speed transmission in my Formula, which is getting a 3.4-liter engine transplant right now. <BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Cooter||03-23-2001||08:19 PM||||Hey 88, how about running the Isuzu tranny with a 3.73 or 4.10 gearset and a stock 3.4 for me. I like the closer ratios of the Isuzu and think it would be a good combination. Thanks-<BR>Greg||||reg||1
Z||000011||88formula||03-23-2001||09:31 PM||||Isuzu with the 3.73 final drive I got 0-60 mph in 6.55 seconds and the quarter mile in 14.77 seconds @ 91.6 mph.<P>Isuzu with the 3.35 final drive I got 0-60 mph in 6.45 seconds and the quarter mile in 14.78 seconds @ 91.3 mph.<P>I think that covers all the manual transmissions.<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Cooter||03-23-2001||09:50 PM||||Thanks- again! I guess that I will save the money on the gearset and waste it on something else. I have a couple of good Isuzu's laying around, but I aint messing with it unless the Muncie blows out. But that could happen with a little bit of boost on the old 3.4 . . . .||||reg||1
Z||000013||owenslee||03-23-2001||10:57 PM||||To all who have responded...Thanks!<P>The replys were well thought out and I am reconsidering the Getrag.<P>Formula 88...if it is not too much hassle, how would the Getrag times compare if the optional 3.94 final drive were installed?<P>Several have commented how the 4.10 in the Muncie felt much stronger in acceleration, yet your results show minimal difference.<P>What shift points did you use? <P>Does your program indicate when tire spin occurs as is done in the "Quarter" program? If it does, I would be curious on the effect of a wider tire on 60 foot times.<P>A number of years ago I used the Quarter program to develope a combination in a V-8  Mid engined Corvair. Tire selection was critical to success in that application with 60% of the weight on the rear axle.( a situation very similar in the Fiero) The final result was a car that hooked so hard it would pull the front tires with DOT legal street tires. Unfortunately it also twisted half shaft u-joints in half...not to mention one transmission case cracked...and one differential case split...but it WAS a hard charger...!<P>Kerry<P><P>------------------<BR>"Badges?...Badges..??? We don gat to sho yu no stinkin' BADGES..!!!"||||reg||14
Z||000014||88formula||03-25-2001||01:32 AM||||I'll get back to this one. Don't have time right now.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||88formula||03-25-2001||10:35 PM||||I used all the same launch and shift rpm’s for each gear. I didn't take the time to customize each one because if I did I would be here for hours. I used a launch rpm of 2500 and shifted @ 6000 rpm. Dragstrip does indicate if there is tire spin and allot more information too. Tire spin is the reason why the transmission with the 4.10 gears shows low times. The 4.10 gear produces excessive tire sin. <P>I did a comparison with the two gear ratios in the Muncie transmission. I used a launch rpm of 3500 with sticky 7.5 inch tires a 1-2 shift @ 6000 rpm 2-3 shift @ 5800 rpm 3-4 shift @ 6000 rpm. I put them in the following order.  Lateral g, Seconds, Time spent in each g.<BR>3.65 gear<BR>Lateral g-------seconds---time<BR>Rollout-.61-----.346------2.662<BR>60-foot-.47------2.168----3.564<BR>330-----.27------6.07------8.080<BR>660-----.21------9.357-----5.70<BR>1000----.17------12.166---0<BR>1320----.12------14.530   <P>4.10 gear<BR>Rollout-.66------.332-----2.219<BR>60-foot-.51------.2.122---2.875<BR>330-----.26------.6.028---6.153<BR>660-----.21------9.299----3.575<BR>1000---.15------12.100---0<BR>1320---.14------14.49<P>The car with the 3.65 gear hardly spins the tires but the car with the 4.10 gears spins the tires for a good five feet. If you launch at a lower rpm the 0-60 mph and quarter mile times drop off but the later g from rollout increase a little. As you can see by the lateral acceleration numbers the car with the 4.10 gears shows that it pulls much harder than the car with the 3.65 gears. This is why members on the forum with the 4.10 gears say that there cars pull much harder than they do with 3.65 gears. You can also see that the time spent in each gear also has an effect on the acceleration times. With the 4.10 gears you have to shift more often in the beginning, which wastes time. So just because the 4.10 gears don’t produce good numbers compared to the 3.65 gears doesn’t mean the car with the 4.10 gears doesn’t pull harder because it does.  For an all out drag car the 4.10 gears don’t really match the torque curve of the 3.4-liter engine well. Engines with allot of torque don’t need very low geared final drives to produce good times especially if the motor makes most of its power down low in the rpm band. I have seen Big block drag cars with blowers that had 2.73 final drive gears pull some amazing 60-foot times and quarter mile times.  <P>With 3.94 gears in the Getrag transmission and using the same launch and shift times as the first times it would go 0-60 mph in 6.55 seconds and run the quarter mile in 14.77 seconds @ 91.4 mph. The 60-foot would be 2.15 seconds and you would spin your tires for about three feet. The best shift times and launch rpm with sticky 7.5 inch tires for that transmission and 3.94 gears would be 3500-rpm launch, 1-2 @ 6000 rpm, 2-3 @ 5800 rpm, and 3-4 @ 6000 rpm. That would produce a 0-60 mph time of 6.41 seconds and the quarter mile would come in 17.66 seconds @ 91.5 mph. The 60-foot time would be 2.076 with a .70 later g upon rollout. Using even sticker wider tires will of course improve those times by eliminating tire spin as long as you don’t increase reciprocating weight at the same time by too much. <BR>    <BR>            <BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
